\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For \(n\in\mathbb N\), let
\(\sigma(n)=\sum_{d\mid n,\ d>0}d\) be the sum of all its positive
divisors.  The integer \(n\) is perfect when \(\sigma(n)=2n\),
equivalently when it equals the sum of its proper positive divisors.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is there any odd integer \(n>1\) satisfying
\[
 \sigma(n)=2n?
\]
Equivalently, is the set of odd perfect numbers empty? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19746}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does an odd perfect number exist?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1171 (NT-026). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Pascal Ochem and Joshua Zelinsky, \emph{On odd perfect numbers with exactly one even exponent greater than 2}, arXiv:2607.19746 (2026), Section 1, definition of an odd perfect number. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19746}.
\end{description}
